WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.539
A grader is a function, so write it like one.

2
00:00:02.819 --> 00:00:09.453
Week 1 ended with failure modes: concrete ways your system goes wrong, found by reading real outputs.

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:20.507
A grader turns one of those failure modes into a function: it takes an output (and sometimes the input and a reference answer) and returns a score, usually pass or fail.

4
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.179
An eval is a set of cases plus the graders that score them.

5
00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:28.128
There are three kinds of grader, and the order you reach for them matters.

6
00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:42.637
Anthropic’s guide to building evals ranks them plainly: code-based grading is the fastest, most reliable, and most scalable, but lacks nuance; human grading is the most flexible and highest quality, but slow and expensive;

7
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:49.189
LLM-based grading is fast and flexible but has to be tested for reliability before you scale it.

8
00:00:49.469 --> 00:01:05.853
Anthropic’s engineering team makes the same trade-off explicit in its write-up on agent evals: code graders are cheap, objective, reproducible, and easy to debug, and their weakness is brittleness when a valid answer looks different from what the code expects.

9
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:15.797
The practical rule follows: make every check you can deterministic, and save model judges (next lesson) for criteria code genuinely can’t decide.

10
00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:22.392
A deterministic grader gives the same answer every run, costs nothing, and can itself be unit-tested.

11
00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:24.592
That last property is underrated.

12
00:01:24.872 --> 00:01:30.845
A grader is code that decides whether you ship, so it deserves tests as much as anything in your product.

13
00:01:31.125 --> 00:01:46.827
This lesson works through the toolbox from simplest to most involved: exact and normalized matching, regular expressions, fuzzy matching, schema checks, executing generated code, and reference-overlap metrics, with what each one can and can’t tell you.
